 and applied in array order; the first error throws immediately without evaluating later operations
 or exposing partial changes.
 
+`createPatch(from, to)` produces a JSON Patch that transforms one JSON document into another:
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+createPatch({ users: [{ name: "Ada" }] }, { users: [{ name: "Grace" }] });
+// [{ op: "replace", path: "/users/0/name", value: "Grace" }]
+```
+
+Returns an array of `add`, `remove`, and `replace` operations (typed as `PatchOperation[]`).
+Equal documents produce an empty array; object member order does not matter. Changes descend
+into objects and arrays rather than replacing whole containers. Unchanged array elements are
+aligned so insertions and removals do not resend the elements that shift position. Values of
+different types are replaced at their location, using the empty path for a root replacement.
+The patch is not guaranteed to have the globally smallest operation count or serialized size.
+Both inputs must satisfy the JSON requirements above; invalid inputs throw errors. Neither
+input is modified, and values included in the patch are deep copies with no input references.
+
 `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies JSON Merge Patch as defined by
 [RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386.html):
 
